## 4. Scene model (v1)

```ts
type Actor = "human" | "agent";
type BoxKind = "room" | "volume";

type Box = {
  id: string;        // server-assigned, per kind: "room_1", "room_2", "volume_1", ...
  kind: BoxKind;     // room = hollow (floor + walls, no ceiling); volume = solid
  x: number;         // footprint min corner, meters
  z: number;
  width: number;     // along +x, > 0
  depth: number;     // along +z, > 0
  height: number;    // >= 0.05 (one vertical snap step)
  createdBy: Actor;
};

type View = {
  focus: { x: number; z: number };                             // ground point under the screen center
  yaw: number;                                                 // degrees, 0..360
  bounds: { x: number; z: number; width: number; depth: number }; // axis-aligned box around the visible ground
};

type Scene = { view: View; boxes: Box[] };
```

- **Box** is the umbrella term, and **room** and **volume** are its two kinds. They share every field and differ in meaning and rendering. A room will later get openings (doors, windows, holes), and a volume is something you stand on or bump into.
- **Default heights:** room 3 m, volume 0.25 m, used when the drawer (human or agent) doesn't give one.
- **IDs are never reused.** Counters only go up. Undoing a creation removes the box, and redoing it brings it back **with the same ID**, because the inverse stores the full box.
- **`view` changes shape.** With a rotated camera, the visible ground is a rotated quad, not an axis-aligned rect. We report the focus point, the yaw and the axis-aligned bounds of the visible ground. That's enough for the agent to put things on screen. As before, setting the view is not an edit, isn't broadcast and never enters the history.
- Selection is **editor-local** state and is not part of the scene.

